A||||10||baggy||code 12||||1||
Z||000000||baggy||01-23-2000||01:44 PM||||thats all I get is code twelve..<BR>am Idoing it right?<BR>stick the jumper wire into the lower left to sockets...<BR>am I supposed to let the engine run while testing??<BR>do I have to reset the ECM??||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ed||01-23-2000||04:23 PM||||From the shop manual diagnostic circuit check:<P>Ignition "on"  Engine "off"<P>note "service engine soon"  (ses) light<P>jumper ALDL (that's your connections next to the cigarette lighter) terminals B to A<P>Does ses light flash code 12 <P>etc. <P>No other codes means no codes from your computer sensors were set. <P>You don't have to do anything else to check codes or get back to business. .  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||David Gamache||01-23-2000||04:25 PM||||The code 12 is nornal. Only if there is a problem will the engine set additional codes. It is only necessary to"clear" the ECM<BR>when additional codes have appeared and repairs have been made. This can be accomplished by disconnecting the battery for a few seconds or pulling the ECM fuse. If you were grounding the wrong connections you would not have even gotten the code 12.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||01-23-2000||04:38 PM||||<BR>If you're new at this, I have one piece of advice -<P><B>DON'T CRANK THE ENGINE WHILE YOU HAVE THE JUMPER WIRE INSERTED IN THE ALCL CONNECTOR!!!</B><P>Sorry for shouting, but I wanted to make sure you heard me.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000004||baggy||01-23-2000||06:04 PM||||I've already crnked it..<BR>what is that gonna do to it?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||01-23-2000||06:11 PM||||It won't hurt anything.  That is what you have to do to set the base timing.||||reg||1
Z||000006||baggy||01-23-2000||06:52 PM||||ok, since no other codes came up, that means my engine bucking is mechanical..any tips?<BR>it starts bucking and jerking when I try and cruise at 40 to 50 mph..<BR>if I keep it under a steady pull it doesnt do it..<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||01-23-2000||07:33 PM||||Doesn't mean it's mechanical only, it just means what ever is causing thep problem isn't bothering the ECM. You can have a vacuum leak and not set a code.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick||01-23-2000||09:05 PM||||<BR>On page 7A-11 of my Haynes Computer Codes book, it states:<P><B>Caution: <I>Don't crank the engine with the diagnostic terminal grounded - the ECM could be damaged.</I></B><P>Carrolles, are you stating that this warning is incorrect for all Fieros?||||reg||5
Z||000009||Oreif||01-23-2000||10:21 PM||||The Pontiac Service Manual States the ECM needs to be grounded then start the engine to set the timing. You cannot damage the ECM if the terminal is grounded. All this does is set the sensor controls to a default setting and does not adjust anything. The only thing I have seen it mess up is the IAC setting which will cause a high idle condition. To reset the High Idle drive car over 40mph for 30 seconds. The ECM resets the IAC and Idle returns to normal.||||reg||4
Z||000010||Patrick||01-23-2000||11:02 PM||||<BR>Thanks Oreif. I just didn't want to see anyone fry their ECM.<P>I wonder why the Haynes book has the warning? <BR>||||reg||14
